Subject: [sshmap fork 0005] grapheme clusters: an emoji sequence must not truncate the chunk

Provenance: upstream pyte master (0.8.3.dev) — the "Fixed rendering of multi code-point emoji
            sequences" change (CHANGES, 0.8.3 Unreleased): the `grapheme_clusters()` generator and
            the cached `wcswidth` in draw()/display(). `wcswidth` is NOT a per-character sum: the
            wcwidth package knows the emoji ZWJ sequences, which is exactly why the GRID needs it
            and why the canvas must read the same table. Reproduced against the vendored 0.8.2 fork
            first: `Screen.draw()` `break`s out of the WHOLE data string on a code point whose
            `wcwidth` is 0 and which is not `unicodedata.combining` — the ZWJ U+200D, the variation
            selectors U+FE0E/U+FE0F — so `feed()` silently drops everything after it
            (`A\u2764\ufe0fB` -> "A\u2764"); no exception and no log line, which is why the ledger
            entry (AUDIT_PENDING.md N26) calls it the silent one.
            TWO deliberate deviations from upstream's version, recorded here because a rebase must
            not "fix" them away:
            (1) the "extend" test inside `grapheme_clusters()` is the whole M category set
                (Mn/Mc/Me) instead of `unicodedata.combining` (canonical combining class != 0).
                A mark whose class IS 0 — a Thai vowel sign, a Devanagari matra, the keycap's
                U+20E3 — became its own zero-width cluster; upstream `break`s the chunk on it and
                loses the tail, and no emulator can draw a mark that is not attached to a base.
                Measured: 'A1\ufe0f\u20e3B' keeps its trailing 'B' here and loses it upstream;
                '\u0e01\u0e31' (Thai) and '\u0915\u093e' (Devanagari) attach to their base instead
                of vanishing.
            (2) the 0.8.2 NFC merge is preserved: the old single-character loop merged a combining
                mark into the PREVIOUS cell with `unicodedata.normalize("NFC", ...)`, so a
                decomposed stream produced the cell `Á`; upstream's cluster version stores the
                decomposed form, which changes what a search over `TerminalScreen.text_lines()`
                finds. A multi-code-point cluster is normalized before it is stored (NFC preserves
                the display width, and the width is recomputed from the normalized value).
            The residual rule that is OURS alone: a zero-width cluster that is not a mark at all
            (U+FEFF, a lone leading ZWJ) is SKIPPED instead of `break`ing the chunk — a terminal
            ignores what it cannot draw, and the tail of the chunk must always survive.
            The canvas half of the contract is NOT in this patch: `modules/terminal_widget.py`
            `char_width()` sums `wcwidth` per code point, so a ZWJ/skin-tone cluster measured 4
            where the grid reserves 2 — it moves to `wcswidth` for a cell that carries more than
            one code point in the same release (v1.5.7.1).
Base:       pyte 0.8.2 (PyPI sdist, sha256 in MANIFEST.md), pyte/screens.py (after patches 0001-0004).
Policy:     drop when the base is raised to an upstream release that carries the emoji fix
            (0.8.3+); (1), (2) and the zero-width skip have no upstream counterpart — keep them, or
            file them upstream.
Applied:    2026-09-26, sshmap v1.5.7.1.

